Consider the four-nucleon system treated as a quantum-mechanical system of four point-like nucleons (two protons and two neutrons) interacting through a nucleon–nucleon (NN) potential, possibly supplemented by a three-nucleon (3N) potential, together with the electromagnetic (Coulomb) interaction between the protons. Within this framework one studies:

(a) the α-particle (⁴He) ground state with quantum numbers J^π = 0⁺, T = 0, whose experimental binding energy is approximately 28.296 MeV;

(b) the triton (³H) threshold, i.e. the p + ³H separation energy of ⁴He;

(c) the first excited state of ⁴He, a J^π = 0⁺ resonance lying just above the p + ³H threshold (and below the n + ³He threshold), characterized by a resonance energy E_r and width Γ; this resonance dominates the very-low-energy p + ³H elastic excitation function and the singlet (S = 0) p–³H scattering length below the opening of the n + ³He channel.

The p + ³H and n + ³He thresholds are nearly degenerate and carry the same isospin quantum numbers; their splitting arises only from the Coulomb interaction and the proton–neutron mass difference.

Problem. Exhibit a realistic nuclear interaction (an NN potential together, if necessary, with a 3N potential) that, when used in an exact four-body scattering/bound-state calculation incorporating the proper Coulomb treatment that separates the p + ³H and n + ³He channels, simultaneously reproduces:

(i) the experimental ⁴He ground-state binding energy;
(ii) the experimental p + ³H threshold (equivalently, the ³H binding energy);
(iii) the experimental position E_r and width Γ of the first excited 0⁺ resonance of ⁴He relative to the p + ³H threshold,

so that the resulting very-low-energy p + ³H excitation function and singlet p–³H scattering length below the n + ³He threshold agree with experiment. Determine whether such an interaction exists and, if so, construct it; otherwise quantify the obstruction.

